Create an SVG diagram for a technical talk, for the slide "B.1 Guest Drivers
and Their Device Models" (backup chapter B) with the subtitle "Every driver
in the guest has a counterpart in Cloud Hypervisor". Same deck style as
guest-device-access-sequence.svg (1200x675, white background, 'IBM Plex Sans'
/ 'Source Code Pro', green = guest, purple = KVM, blue = VMM, grey = host).
No title or subtitle in the SVG: the slide's markdown heading provides
them, so they match normal slides. Keep the top free (content starts at
y ~ 175) and align the content with the slide text area (x 77.5 -
1122.5).

Very high level, generic device classes instead of concrete products. Four
full-width horizontal layers on the left ~3/4 of the canvas, each with its
name and a short subtitle on the left: Guest VM (guest kernel), KVM (host
kernel, a thin band), cloud-hypervisor (VMM, user space), Host (files and
devices).

Two columns of boxes inside the layers, one per device class:
- "Block device driver" (e.g. virtio-blk) in the guest, "Block device model"
  (emulates the device) in the VMM, disk.img (disk image file) on the host.
- "Network device driver" (e.g. virtio-net) in the guest, "Network device
  model" (emulates the device) in the VMM, tap0 (tap device) on the host.

Make the pairing discoverable: driver and device model of one class share a
colour (block: orange, network: teal) that differs from the layer colours,
sit exactly above each other, and are joined by a dashed line in that colour
that crosses the KVM band. Like puzzle pieces, each driver has a joint sticking
out of its bottom edge and its device model has the matching cut-out in its
top edge, with a different shape per class: zigzag teeth for block, a
half-moon for network. Device models connect to their host backend with a
plain grey line.

Right column: "Driver ↔ device model" - each driver in the guest talks to its
counterpart, a device model in the VMM; both speak the same device interface.

One block access as a red path with numbered badges in the block column, next
to the dashed pairing line:

1. Driver accesses a device register (arrow from the driver into KVM).
2. VM exit: trap into KVM (badge inside the KVM band).
3. KVM_RUN returns to the VMM (arrow from KVM into the block device model).
4. The device model does the I/O on disk.img (arrow down to the host).
5. VM entry: guest continues (dashed arrow from the device model back up to
   the driver, with the badge in the KVM band).

The right column gets a matching numbered list "One block access" below the
driver/device model text.
